{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a local certificate authority on node1 named “OS Comparison Root CA”.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A self-signed certificate and matching 4096-bit RSA private key were created. Direct inspection shows the requested subject and issuer, critical CA:TRUE and certificate-signing extensions, matching key/certificate public keys, and root-owned permissions of 0600 for the key and 0644 for the certificate.","evidence_ids":["cmd-758f2d4b4c104424","cmd-3b09801bd7014c88"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Install the CA certificate as a trusted system certificate authority on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The certificate was placed in the RHEL trust-anchor directory and the system trust store was regenerated. OpenSSL verification returned OK and p11-kit identified the certificate as an authority trust anchor. The same certificate fingerprint and trusted state remained present after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-28c8736bc53b46f3","cmd-3b09801bd7014c88","cmd-0098d1d942e24fbd"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: installing OpenSSL was a necessary prerequisite, creating the key and certificate and updating the trust store implemented the task, and rebooting fulfilled the mandatory evidence protocol. Package health remained good, no failed services or new listeners appeared, and the restart protocol was complete and consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot identities. One minor executor-created residue remained: /tmp/codex-reboot.log, created by the restart command and not cleaned up. Its creation supported the bounded restart mechanism, but retaining it afterward was unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8ea9e2a2d0394330","cmd-758f2d4b4c104424","cmd-28c8736bc53b46f3","cmd-10403457b73141fd","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The provider-wide node-restart protocol is also complete: it captures a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, restored connectivity and services, and persistent CA key, certificate, and trust state after recovery.","limitations":["The contents and permissions of the retained /tmp/codex-reboot.log file were not captured; only its presence is established."]}
